---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import SeaBreak from '../../components/SeaBreak.astro';
import { celestial, ocean } from '../../config/images';
import Breadcrumbs from '../../components/Breadcrumbs.astro';
import CTABand from '../../components/CTABand.astro';
const crumbs = [
  { label: 'Explore Astrology', href: '/explore/' },
  { label: 'Personal Purpose', href: '/explore/personal-purpose/' },
];
const purposeLenses = [
  { label: 'Contribution', question: 'What wants to be expressed or offered?', note: 'Look for repeated themes around visibility, authorship, service, leadership, teaching, making, or other forms of contribution.' },
  { label: 'Conditions', question: 'What helps that expression work?', note: 'Autonomy, collaboration, depth, variety, structure, audience, pace, and environment can matter as much as a job title.' },
  { label: 'Resources', question: 'What supports the work?', note: 'Skills, values, money, stamina, relationships, training, and practical responsibilities all belong in the conversation.' },
  { label: 'Timing', question: 'Why is the question alive now?', note: 'Transits and other timing factors can describe when an old question becomes harder to ignore or newly possible to examine.' },
];
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Personal Purpose. What a chart can suggest about vocation"
  description="Can a birth chart reveal purpose or vocation? Learn how astrologers synthesize the Sun, Midheaven, houses, Saturn, nodes, and other factors without pretending the chart contains one predetermined career."
  path="/explore/personal-purpose/"
  breadcrumbs={crumbs}
>
  <Breadcrumbs crumbs={crumbs} />
  <section class="hero hero--split">
    <div class="container">
      <div>
      <p class="eyebrow">Explore Astrology</p>
      <h1>Personal purpose and the chart</h1>
      <p class="lede">
        “What am I here to do?” sounds like one question, but it usually contains several: What
        matters to me? What kind of contribution fits? What am I ready to develop now? A chart can
        add perspective to those questions. I would not ask it to hand you one predetermined answer.
      </p>
      </div>
      <img class="hero__img" src={ocean.floatingInTrust.src} srcset={ocean.floatingInTrust.srcset} sizes="100vw" alt={ocean.floatingInTrust.alt} width={ocean.floatingInTrust.width} height={ocean.floatingInTrust.height} style="object-position: center 22%;" fetchpriority="high" />
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="container prose flow">
      <h2>Start with four lenses, not one placement</h2>
      <p>
        Purpose is easier to think about when the question is broken into parts. These are not four
        astrological rules. They are four lenses that help keep the chart connected to the life being
        lived.
      </p>
      <div class="purpose-grid">
        {purposeLenses.map((lens) => (
          <article class="purpose-card">
            <p class="eyebrow">{lens.label}</p>
            <h3>{lens.question}</h3>
            <p>{lens.note}</p>
          </article>
        ))}
      </div>
      <div class="callout">
        <p style="margin: 0;">
          <strong>A pattern is not a profession.</strong> A chart might repeat themes of communication,
          autonomy, and public visibility. That still does not equal “you should become a writer.” It
          gives us qualities we can compare against several real options.
        </p>
      </div>

      <h2>There is no single “purpose placement”</h2>
      <p>
        This is exactly the kind of question that shows why synthesis matters. Astrologers may look
        at the Sun, Midheaven, tenth house, second and sixth houses, Saturn, the lunar nodes, their
        rulers, aspects, and other chart factors. None of them is a job-title generator. Together,
        they can describe recurring themes around contribution, motivation, skill, visibility,
        responsibility, livelihood, and development.
      </p>
      <p>
        I am interested in where those signals repeat or reinforce one another. One placement can be
        interesting; several parts of the chart pointing toward the same kind of need or capacity
        deserve more attention.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <SeaBreak image={celestial.stairway} />

  <section class="section section--sea">
    <div class="container prose flow">
      <h2>Purpose is larger than occupation</h2>
      <p>
        A chart does not have to culminate in a career answer. The same underlying pattern can be
        lived through paid work, family responsibility, creative practice, teaching, service,
        leadership, study, community, or several of those across a lifetime. Meaningful work and a
        meaningful life overlap, but they are not identical.
      </p>
      <p>
        That is why I prefer questions about qualities and conditions over “Which profession does my
        chart say I should choose?” Does the person need autonomy? Depth? Variety? An audience?
        Mastery? Direct usefulness? Room to create? Those answers can help someone evaluate several
        real options without pretending astrology has chosen one for them.
      </p>

      <h2>Timing changes the question</h2>
      <p>
        Questions about purpose often become louder during particular periods. A transit or
        progression can bring an old dissatisfaction to the surface, put new responsibility in
        front of you, or make a direction that once fit feel less convincing. That does not mean the
        sky has issued an instruction. It may mean the question itself has become timely.
      </p>
      <p>
        See <a href="/explore/transits/">transits</a>, or bring the whole question to a
        <a href="/readings/natal/">natal reading</a>, where the natal pattern and current conditions
        can be considered together.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <CTABand
    heading="Bring the question, not a demand for one answer"
    body="Purpose questions become more useful when the whole chart is read alongside the life you are actually choosing among. If that is the question you are carrying now, the Reading Finder can help you decide where to begin."
    buttonLabel="Use the Reading Finder"
    buttonHref="/reading-finder/"
    secondaryLabel="About the Natal Reading"
    secondaryHref="/readings/natal/"
  />

  <style>
    .purpose-grid {
      display: grid;
      grid-template-columns: 1fr;
      gap: var(--space-4);
      margin-block: var(--space-6);
    }
    .purpose-card {
      padding: var(--space-5);
      border: 1px solid var(--border-color, currentColor);
      border-radius: var(--radius-lg, 1rem);
    }
    .purpose-card h3,
    .purpose-card p:last-child { margin-bottom: 0; }
    @media (min-width: 768px) {
      .purpose-grid { grid-template-columns: repeat(2, minmax(0, 1fr)); }
    }
  </style>
</BaseLayout>
